# Clean Claude Orphans

Reconciles installed Claude Code state (MCP servers, plugins, plugin marketplaces, skills)
against `{{ .chezmoi.sourceDir -}}/.chezmoidata/packages.yaml`, starting from
`audit-packages`'s findings. For every orphan, decide **REMOVE**, **KEEP**, or **DECLARE**
— never remove anything without confirming the full plan with the user first.

Use the `chezmoi` skill to add a DECLARE item back into `packages.yaml`.

## Step 1: Gather orphans and environment context

```bash
~/.claude/skills/clean-claude-orphans/scripts/list-claude-orphans.sh
```

The script is a chezmoi template — this repo's root is baked into it at `chezmoi apply`
time (`dir .chezmoi.sourceDir`, since `.chezmoi.sourceDir` resolves to `home/` per
`.chezmoiroot`, not the repo root), so it needs no arguments.

Runs `audit-packages` and extracts the four Claude Code orphan sections as clean
`<category>\t<item>` lines (`mcp_server`, `plugin`, `marketplace`, `skill`), plus context
needed before classifying anything:

- `## environments` — every `~/.claude*` environment directory found on disk.
- `## chezmoi-personal-plugins` — this repo's own marketplace catalog
  (`{{ dir .chezmoi.sourceDir -}}/.claude-plugin/marketplace.json`), one line per plugin with
  its bundled skills (if any) in column 2. Use this to recognize items already declared by
  the marketplace itself — see Step 3.
- `## chezmoi-managed-skills` — every skill directory under
  `{{ .chezmoi.sourceDir -}}/dot_claude/skills/`, deployed by `chezmoi apply` rather
  than `npx skills add`.
- `## installed-plugins` — every currently installed plugin's `scope` and (for
  project/local scope) owning `projectPath`, needed before uninstalling anything — see
  Step 4.

Read-only — changes nothing.

If the user already pasted `audit-packages` output, use that instead of re-running it, but
still run the script (or read the three files above directly) to get the marketplace,
chezmoi-managed-skills, and installed-plugins context it doesn't include.
`audit-packages` doesn't dedupe its comparison lists, so the same plugin can appear once
per project that enables it — collapse identical lines to one item before presenting a plan.

## Step 2: Know the environment model before touching anything

- A machine may have several Claude Code environments (`~/.claude`, `~/.claude-work`,
  `~/.claude-personal`, ...) selected via `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`
  (`openspec/specs/claude-environments/spec.md`).
- **Skills are shared** across every environment via a symlink
  (`~/.claude-<name>/skills -> ~/.claude/skills`) — removing one removes it from all of them.
- **MCP servers and plugins are NOT shared** — each environment has independent state, and
  a `claude` invocation only sees the environment named by its own `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`. To
  check or clean a non-current environment, prefix every `claude` command with
  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<env_dir>` (e.g. `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude-work claude plugins list
  --json`), using the environment dirs Step 1 found. Don't declare a machine "clean" after
  only checking the current environment.
- Plugins also have an **enablement scope** separate from install scope: installed at user,
  project, or local scope, but a project can *enable* it independently via
  `.claude/settings.json` (`enabledPlugins`, tracked/shared) or `.claude/settings.local.json`
  (gitignored, personal). `packages.yaml` only records the user-scope install baseline — it
  says nothing about project/local enablement. Before uninstalling a plugin, check whether
  any project you know of still enables it; if so, uninstalling breaks that project's next
  session. Step 1's `## installed-plugins` section already tells you each plugin's scope and
  owning project — see Step 4 for what to do with it.

## Step 3: Classify each orphan

Work marketplaces last — removing one can orphan the plugins installed from it.

### MCP servers
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Still declared in a project's own `.mcp.json` you use | **DECLARE** in packages.yaml |
| Duplicates a server a chezmoi-personal marketplace plugin already registers (`## chezmoi-personal-plugins` from Step 1 — e.g. `browser-tools`, `aws-local-dev`, `openspec-dashboard`) | **REMOVE** the manual entry; re-adding later is a one-line `enabledPlugins` toggle for that plugin, not a reinstall |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `claude mcp remove <name>` |

### Plugins
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| `.id` ends `@chezmoi-personal` (cross-check `## chezmoi-personal-plugins` from Step 1) | **NEVER DECLARE** — already declared by this repo's own marketplace manifest, outside packages.yaml. If genuinely unused, **REMOVE** via `claude plugins uninstall` (see Step 4 for scope handling), but never suggest a packages.yaml entry for it |
| Still enabled in any project's `.claude/settings.json` or `.claude/settings.local.json` | **KEEP** (or **DECLARE** if it should be standard, and not `@chezmoi-personal`-sourced) |
| A chezmoi-personal marketplace plugin already covers the same functionality under a different name/marketplace (`## chezmoi-personal-plugins` from Step 1) | **REMOVE**, recommend switching to the chezmoi-personal one — re-adding is a one-line enable, not a fresh install |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `claude plugins uninstall <id>` (see Step 4 for scope handling) |

### Plugin marketplaces
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Name is `chezmoi-personal`, or its `source` is a local directory (no `repo`/`url`) resolving to this repo's root | **NEVER an orphan** — it's this repo's own marketplace, registered outside packages.yaml's `plugin_marketplaces` list (see `openspec/specs/claude-plugin-marketplace/spec.md`). `audit-packages` reports it as a literal `null` orphan line (its jq query has no `repo`/`url` field to read for a local-directory source) — ignore any `null` line rather than acting on it. |
| Still has any KEPT plugin installed from it (`claude plugins list --json`, `.id` ends `@<marketplace>`) | **KEEP** until those plugins are removed first |
| No remaining plugins, not chezmoi-personal | **REMOVE**: `claude plugins marketplace remove <name>` |

### Skills
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Directory name appears in `## chezmoi-managed-skills` from Step 1 (lives at `{{ .chezmoi.sourceDir -}}/dot_claude/skills/<name>`) | **KEEP** — deployed by `chezmoi apply`, never installed via `npx skills add`; never DECLARE or REMOVE it through this skill |
| Referenced by name inside another skill or plugin you're keeping | **KEEP** — it's a dependency, not an orphan |
| Duplicates a skill a chezmoi-personal marketplace plugin bundles (`## chezmoi-personal-plugins` column 2 from Step 1 — e.g. `code-quality`, `systems-design`) | **REMOVE** the standalone copy, keep the plugin-provided one — re-adding is a one-line plugin enable, not a reinstall |
| `audit-packages`'s wildcard-prefix match is a heuristic, not ground truth | Skim the skill's own `SKILL.md`/README before trusting a "covered by collection spec" note |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `rm -rf ~/.claude/skills/<name>` (affects every environment — Step 2) |

## Step 3.5: Unclutter — duplicates beyond the orphan list

The checks above also apply outside the orphan list: a plugin, skill, or MCP server can
duplicate something a chezmoi-personal plugin already provides while still being correctly
declared in packages.yaml (so `audit-packages` never flags it). Skim what's actually
installed — `claude plugins list --json`, `ls ~/.claude/skills/`, `claude mcp list` — against
Step 1's `## chezmoi-personal-plugins` catalog, not just the `## orphans` section.

A match is a decluttering candidate, not just an orphan: offer to **REMOVE** it and note
that re-adding it later is a one-line `enabledPlugins` toggle in whatever project needs it
(Step 2), not a packages.yaml edit or a fresh install. Fold these into the same
confirm-then-execute plan as Step 4 — don't run this as a separate pass.

## Step 4: Present the plan, confirm, then execute

List every orphan (and Step 3.5 candidate) with its verdict and a one-line reason. Ask the
user to confirm the whole plan, or adjust specific items, before running anything
destructive.

Within a category, remove plugins sourced from a marketplace before removing that
marketplace. For DECLARE items, edit `packages.yaml` (via the `chezmoi` skill) instead of
touching installed state.

### Removing a plugin: scope determines the procedure

Step 1's `## installed-plugins` section already tells you each plugin's `scope` and (for
project/local scope) `projectPath` — don't rediscover this by trial and error:

- **`user` scope**: `claude plugins uninstall <id>` works directly, from anywhere.
- **`project` or `local` scope**: `claude plugins uninstall <id>` fails until the
  `enabledPlugins` entry for it is removed from the owning project's `.claude/settings.json`
  (project scope, tracked with the team) or `.claude/settings.local.json` (local scope,
  gitignored) — the file lives at the `projectPath` Step 1 reported. Remove that entry, then
  re-run `claude plugins uninstall <id> --scope <project|local>` with your working directory
  inside `projectPath` — scope resolution is cwd-relative, so running it from elsewhere
  fails even with the right flag.

## Step 5: Verify

Re-run `audit-packages` (or the Step 1 script). Anything still listed should have an
explanation — most often a plugin still enabled by a project that wasn't checked.

## Common mistakes

- Uninstalling a plugin without checking project-level `enabledPlugins` first.
- Uninstalling a project/local-scope plugin with a bare `claude plugins uninstall <id>` and
  no `--scope` flag, or from outside its owning `projectPath` — both fail. See Step 4.
- Suggesting DECLARE for a plugin already covered by the `chezmoi-personal` marketplace
  (`## chezmoi-personal-plugins` in Step 1) — it doesn't belong in packages.yaml at all.
- Treating a chezmoi-managed skill (`## chezmoi-managed-skills` in Step 1) as an
  `npx skills add` orphan needing DECLARE.
- Removing the `chezmoi-personal` marketplace, or acting on its literal `null` orphan line.
- Treating a skill orphan as machine-local — removing it removes it from every
  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` environment at once.
- Checking only the current `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` and calling the machine clean while
  sibling environments still carry orphans.
- Only scanning the `## orphans` list and missing Step 3.5 duplicates that are correctly
  declared but still redundant with a chezmoi-personal plugin.
